plan ApplicationsAndActors
title   Closed applications and owned actor lifetimes
status  proposed pending approval
goal
    Let consumers compose exact machines, install complete services, and operate independent actors
    through one Runtime with truthful ownership, context and snapshots. This proposed Plan owns
    composition, requirements, Implementation, readiness, refs, admission, leases, lifecycle and
    context. Statechart behavior belongs to @plan.Statecharts.O1 through @plan.Statecharts.O4;
    operation families keep their own data and work contracts. No approval or completion is claimed.
outcomes
    O1  Applications describe one immutable admitted universe
        module({ id, machines }) is inert tooling metadata with a unique app-local ID and exact
        keyed machine record. app({ id, persistenceVersion, modules }) has explicit identity,
        ordered unaliased modules and exact flattened App.M. Preserve machine property names.
        Reject duplicate property names, durable machine IDs, machine values and tooling owners,
        even under different ergonomic keys. Compilation creates no actor and starts no work.

        App, module, machine, descriptor and stable-ref IDs follow exact name/UTF-8 rules under
        @plan.Statecharts.O1. Every local, shared and Story-local actor uses an exact App.M machine.
        Every listed machine contributes graph edges, named descriptors and inferred requirements.

        The compiled graph is closed for the Runtime lifetime. Module IDs group CLI slices, traces,
        inspection, artifacts and diffs; they are not machine, actor, context, Runtime, persistence
        or operation identity. Moving/renaming tooling ownership breaks artifacts without silent
        alias/compare.

        Runtime/persistence identity remains compatible; historical artifacts require migration.

        A Machine ID is durable machine/artifact identity; an App.M property is a TypeScript key.
        Keep actor ref, actor incarnation, descriptor/K, Story recipe object identity and RunLocalId
        separate. Actor incarnation identifies one Runtime lifetime and never reuses an earlier
        lifetime's occurrence or issue authority.

        A recipe may produce several incarnations. Resource key canonicalization and P/K separation
        belong to @plan.Resources.O1.
    O2  Complete typed Implementations supply services inertly
        RequirementsOf exposes the exact covariant application requirement union without provider
        branding. Descriptors retain raw R. Remove only Flow-owned Scope.Scope from consumer
        requirements. The same law applies to resource, transaction, stream, Machine, Module, App,
        fixture, Story and model values. Their private requirement carriers are covariant.
        RequirementsOf on an inferred fixture, Story or model preserves its exact requirement union;
        supplying complete providers does not replace that union with never or provider branding.

        Machines combine reachable named descriptors/provider graph requirements; modules combine
        their exact machine records; apps combine App.M. Passive reads and callbacks add no
        requirements. Context selectors add typed provider edges, not parentage.

        Preserve exact covariant requirement propagation from descriptor to machine binding to
        module to app. Public Machine does not expose full authored config as a recursive generic.
        RequirementsOf must preserve the union without user wrappers, implementation branding or
        excessive-instantiation failures in accepted isolated consumers. Private carrier
        representation remains a later Spec choice. Reject cyclic/unbounded requirement expansion
        and child-machine edges.

        Implementation is both value and type. Its only value members are succeed, effect and merge.
        succeed(service, value) supplies a complete synchronous non-failing provider.
        effect(service, acquire) acquires at most once per Runtime; Runtime owns its scope and
        finalizer. Its typed acquisition error is ImplementationError. merge composes providers
        without changing service identity.

        Duplicate service identity rejects, never last-wins. Construction acquires nothing.
        RuntimeSetup must close RequirementsOf<App>; complete provider supersets are valid only with
        no unresolved input requirements.

        Fixture Implementations override by exact service identity and preserve authored
        input/success/failure/cancellation/lifetime. They supply environment behavior, not kernels,
        admission, completion, canonical writes, projections or evidence. Reject partial providers,
        remaining requirements, opaque Layer-like replacement semantics, matchers, control
        registries and kernel replacement. No additional public Implementation method is implied.
    O3  Construction is inert and readiness owns one shared bootstrap
        runtimeSetup names an exact App and complete Implementation unless requirements are never.
        Optional persistence is one inert provider under @plan.Persistence.O1 and does not affect
        RequirementsOf<App>. Discovery and construct() are synchronous, service-free and inert: no
        storage, service acquisition, actors, registration, work or evidence.

        The exact consumer call forms are runtimeSetup({ app }) when requirements are never, or
        runtimeSetup({ app, implementation }) with full closure. They preserve RuntimeSetup<A, IE>;
        app-only IE is never. Optional persistence adds a Persistence provider, not a requirement.
        [The API examples](references/core-api.examples.ts) illustrate the final target syntax.

        ready() is the sole public readiness Effect. First execution starts one Runtime-owned
        bootstrap; concurrent/later calls join its same cached terminal Exit without retry.
        Interrupting one caller interrupts only its wait.

        Runtime disposal cancels shared bootstrap, closes admission and prevents late acquisition
        from exposing a handle. Full Runtime readiness exposes Effect<void, Diagnostic>, preserving
        defect/interruption Cause classes. Provider acquisition failure is readiness failure, never
        an operation outcome.

        Bootstrap follows this selected sequence:

        1. Read, decode, validate and prepare detached persistence data.
        2. Acquire Implementation.
        3. Install prepared store, actors and permitted operation facts; providers precede 
        consumers.
        4. Initialize fresh admissions and exact silent provider-derived context baselines.
        5. Seal the graph, activate actors, then expose handles.

        Detached preparation installs no store, initializes no actor and registers none.

        Initial ensures and fixed bindings are complete before activation. No ordinary handle,
        lease, command or partial evidence escapes readiness; an inert prepared React handle is the
        sole exception under @plan.ReactAndHosts.O2.

        Validate provider closure, codec, App provenance, refs, input, bindings, tombstones, cycles,
        persisted identity/version/bounds/context closure and terminal counter headroom before
        installation/activation. Failure closes admission and rolls acquired owners back in reverse
        order. Disposal winning the race leaves terminal disposed truth. Internal phases are
        private; expose no phase union, second readiness API, boot, decoder or mutable hydration.
    O4  Exact refs and admission grant one owner lease
        actorRef(machine, id, { persist?: boolean }) is inert stable identity with exactly machine,
        id and persist. persist defaults false and is metadata, not identity. Its durable wire form
        is `actor:<utf8ByteLength>:<machine-ID><utf8ByteLength>:<authored-stable-ID>`. Each length
        counts that exact segment's UTF-8 bytes.
        Refs are branded to an exact Machine, not an App; persisted App identity qualifies them.
        A ref carries no input, bindings, callbacks, subscription or disposal authority.

        ensureActor(ref, { input, contextBindings? }) restores or creates one stable shared actor
        and returns { actor, dispose }. Each stable identity per Runtime has one shared lease and
        actor lifetime, without caller refcounts. Concurrent ensures join the same admission and
        outcome.

        The first accepted input wins; later input is ignored. Conflicting fixed bindings reject
        without changing the winning actor/input/bindings. Dropping a lease does not dispose.
        getActor(ref) is lookup-only: it creates/adopts nothing, acquires no lease and grants no
        disposal. createActor(machine, options?) creates a fresh opaque Runtime-local actor and
        lease, attached/active before exposure.

        It accepts no stable ID.

        Before registration/store mutation, validate exact App/Machine/ref provenance, fresh input,
        complete bindings, compatible available providers, tombstones, duplicate instance identity,
        cycles and capacity. Install a silent context baseline and logical edges, attach/activate,
        then expose the lease last. Before visibility, any failure reverses staged work, edges,
        mailbox, registry and prepared actor in reverse order.

        No partial snapshot, handle, generation or accepted evidence escapes. Concurrent callers
        share that original failure. If rollback succeeds cleanly, a later ensure may begin a fresh
        attempt with new input.

        A failure after visibility uses ordinary production disposal and retains cleanup truth.

        Lookup/ensure reject missing, foreign, mismatched, closing, disposed or quarantined identity
        with canonical boundary diagnostics as applicable. Local opaque refs cannot become durable
        stable refs or satisfy stable lookup obligations. Boot-restored stable actors
        receive Runtime ownership before escape and remain owned even without a repeated ensure.
    O5  Lifecycle preserves continuity and terminal cleanup truth
        Actor lifecycle is exactly prepared, active, suspended or disposed. Prepared actors are
        inert and buffer bounded commands. Active actors admit commands and own attachments.

        Suspended actors preserve continuity, reject commands and own no live attachments. Disposed
        actors are terminal. Only owner leases or whole-Runtime shutdown dispose; handles/refs grant
        none.

        Disposal is asynchronous, idempotent and joins one cached result. Runtime disposal subsumes
        leases and closes all admission before reverse-dependency cleanup.

        Individual provider disposal rejects before beginning while active or suspended consumers
        remain bound. Name every dependent ref and binding key; leave the provider lifetime and
        admission unchanged. No new edge may attach after provider close begins.

        Stable terminal refs are tombstoned for that Runtime incarnation; another Runtime may reuse
        the identity. Failed cleanup quarantines the identity. get, ensure and repeated dispose
        retain/report the same failure without rerunning cleanup. FlowDisposeError has _tag
        FlowDisposeError, scope actor | runtime and complete Cause.Cause<unknown> at that final host
        boundary.

        Ordinary suspension closes admission at one linearization point.
        It drains pre-close commands FIFO. A blocked head context wakeup can delay that drain until
        its upstream settles.
        Suspension does not let later ordinary events overtake the head. Pending suspension cannot
        prevent owner or whole-Runtime terminal disposal from applying the retirement rule below.

        After its drain, suspension detaches registration, fanout, context subscriptions, activities
        and timers. It interrupts unsettled finite work, closes streams and publishes suspended only
        after cleanup. Preserve
        exact ref, handle, state, memory, last valid context, logical provider edges and
        incarnations, operation records/cursors/pending truth and absolute deadlines. Queued finite
        work settles without adapter start.

        Planned release invokes no domain interruption mapper. An adapter ignoring interruption
        retains only facts needed for truthful fenced settlement; no irreversible remote effect is
        described as reversed.

        Resume waits for finalizers, rechecks exact provider identity, reconciles current context
        through the ordinary context path, reacquires continuing work and reinstalls absolute
        deadlines. At most one overdue refresh occurs. Never replay input, initialization, committed
        events, finite work or stream emissions.

        Equal provider selections stay silent. Missing/foreign/tombstoned providers leave the actor
        suspended without substitution/rebind. Cleanup defect blocks resume until owner/Runtime
        disposal.

        Suspension/resume alone creates no attempt, machine-turn revision or TurnRecord.
        @plan.ReactAndHosts.O2 owns attachment.

        Lifecycle publishes a full snapshot before ordered lifecycle evidence. Restore is prepared
        to prepared before start; start is first activation; suspend/resume connect active and
        suspended; dispose is terminal from any non-disposed state. Fresh, restore, attach, lease
        disposal and Runtime disposal have distinct causes.

        No prepare event or synthetic terminal machine turn exists. Shutdown waits for finalizers,
        accepted terminal evidence and provider release; persistence closeout follows
        @plan.Persistence.O5.

        Terminal disposal may retire a blocked context wakeup and remaining queued work without
        waiting for upstream recovery. It closes admission first and uses the existing terminal
        cleanup boundary. Retired ordinary events do not execute, change domain truth, create work
        or produce committed TurnRecords. Settle every waiting private acknowledgment exactly once
        with terminal failure; never acknowledge retired work as a successful decision.

        Preserve the last committed state, memory and valid context in the terminal snapshot.
        Complete owned cleanup, publish ordered terminal lifecycle evidence and drain its accepted
        prefix. Pending suspension or Story cancellation observes the terminal result rather than
        waiting for an upstream that may never resume. Existing dependent-disposal checks still
        apply before individual provider disposal; Runtime disposal still orders consumers first.
    O6  Fixed context propagates causally complete current truth
        Each Definition context slot selects synchronously/purely from one atomic exact provider
        snapshot and binds once to one compatible ActorRef/incarnation for the consumer lifetime.
        Missing, ambiguous, foreign, mismatched, closing, disposed, tombstoned, unresolvable or
        cyclic providers reject atomically before effects. Each provider must be an actual admitted
        actor in the same Runtime with the required compatible typed selector/capability.

        An opaque local actor identity is not by itself an invalid provider. Story recipe
        translation may supply validated local refs through this same production admission path. A
        forged or opaque carrier without verifiable admitted provider authority fails closed; no
        testing bypass is permitted.

        Edges grant no parentage, ownership or command channel. Initial admission/restore installs a
        complete silent provider-derived baseline; initial onContext selections emit no event.
        Suspension retains logical edges and observed provider revisions even after detaching live
        subscriptions.

        Provider changes invalidate dependent consumers in bounded dependency order. Each context
        wakeup applies the latest complete context when it executes, never a stale selected payload.
        Record exact provider refs and observed revisions.

        A dirty consumer receives at most one combined context turn for the settled wave. Context
        completeness is per-consumer causal closure, including shared upstream ancestors; graph-wide
        simultaneous publication is not required. A diamond consumer never combines a new branch
        with unresolved old lineage from their shared cause.

        A branch settling to an Object.is-equal value still resolves causal debt and unblocks
        consumers without fabricating a value-change event.

        A blocked head context wakeup retains FIFO position. Later ordinary events cannot overtake
        it; suspended/failing upstream can hold them.
        This rule governs ordinary decisions and suspension drains. Terminal disposal may retire
        the blocked lane under @O5; it does not execute a later event ahead of that wakeup.
        Equal scalar/non-record selections are silent
        under Object.is.

        Named non-null plain records compare fixed keys fieldwise. Changed selections publish one
        atomic context revision and reconcile continuing plans once. onContext.select receives
        current/previous and emits one typed self-event, false or null. Emitted events enter the
        ordinary mailbox; propagation directly invokes no domain transition, memory update or finite
        action.

        Pending @Q1, the candidate behavior is that provider publication commits even if a consumer
        selector throws. The consumer retains its last valid context, publishes one issue-only
        snapshot, emits no context event and retries only on a newer provider revision. Never retry
        the same bad revision.

        Capture uses completed context waves. Selected suspended debt rejects with retryable
        ConcurrentCapture until explicit resume/recovery; capture neither progresses, clears nor
        silently waits out debt. @plan.Persistence.O3 and @plan.StoriesAndModels.O4 own their
        distinct capture membership.
    O7  Handles and publications expose complete current cuts
        The ordinary handle exposes only exact ref, send(event): void, getSnapshot() and snapshots.
        send synchronously admits the exact typed EventOf<Machine> or reports synchronous admission
        failure. It returns no Promise, Effect, Fiber, snapshot, handle, receipt, acknowledgment or
        subscription. Prepared and active queues have documented finite capacities; prepared
        supports at least 64 commands.

        Full admission rejects AdmissionOverloaded before occurrence, work or state mutation.
        Suspended/disposed actors reject without buffering. Reentrant accepted sends remain FIFO.

        All events, timers, completions, streams, store/context and lifecycle facts use one ordered
        production decision path. No alternate transition engine or lifecycle queue bypasses actor
        ordering.

        Every successful turn exposes one indivisible actor/store cut. An observer cannot see the
        initiating store revision without its committed actor snapshot. Evidence contains only
        committed facts in one Runtime-global order.

        Private acknowledgment settles after the ordered decision; publishing decisions wait for
        immutable evidence acceptance but not sink processing or user Effect settlement. Silent
        no-ops still settle once. Commit through publication/evidence acceptance is
        interruption-safe; after mutation, overflow or disposal cannot leave half a publication.

        Sink behavior belongs to @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O3.

        getSnapshot/snapshots expose complete published current truth: exact active leaf, memory,
        inherited context, lifecycle, active issues, one safe-integer public revision, observed
        store revision, passive named-operation readers and timers. Readers retain their captured
        cut; slow subscribers may skip intermediate snapshots. New subscriptions replay current
        truth without a read/subscribe gap.

        Prepared subscriptions replay the prepared snapshot immediately and continue through
        activation. Suspended subscriptions replay suspended once and complete; they never reopen on
        resume. Disposed subscriptions emit/replay terminal truth then complete.

        A terminal-looking machine leaf does not complete an actor stream.

        Machine, projection-only, lifecycle-only and issue-only publications are all complete.
        Machine turns advance publication and private turn revisions; issue/lifecycle publications
        advance only the defined publication revision. No second public revision, raw Cause,
        receipt, full diagnostic, pending outcome/inventory, handled/error field, child projection,
        nested XState state, final kind or actor output escapes.

        Resource/transaction/stream exact readers remain owned by their capability Plans. Copies
        isolate Flow-owned envelopes, records, arrays, key containers, readers, issues and
        snapshots; opaque application values retain identity. Time is integer epoch milliseconds
        from the Runtime Effect Clock.

        Pre-commit callback defects follow @plan.Statecharts.O3. Invariant defects close admission,
        settle current/buffered acknowledgments, finalize owned work and publish disposed
        fatal/cleanup truth without a fabricated terminal turn. Operational issue clearing
        identifies actor incarnation, source and binding, omitting attempt generation.

        Later success/release by that owner clears earlier operational summaries; another lifetime
        cannot inherit, clear or republish them. Ordered stable diagnostics survive summary
        clearing.

        Revisions, generations, occurrence ordinals and evidence sequences remain nonnegative safe
        integers, never reused. Reserve mandatory terminal headroom before admission/restore; reject
        insufficient capacity before mutation. Ordinary work cannot spend terminal reserves.

        Restore validates high cursors before installation. No post-publication overflow is allowed.
        Capture observes completed committed cuts, not mutable live state, and performs no time
        movement, actor creation/disposal/restore, external work or context progress.
decisions
    D1  One compiled graph and Runtime own production semantics
        Live hosts, requests, React, Stories, tests and CLI use the same compiled admission graph,
        actor execution, canonical operation owners and cleanup rules. Host adapters supply inputs;
        inspection observes; models remain pure. Runtime semantics cannot depend on those adapters.
    D2  Readiness and terminal ownership outlive individual callers
        Shared bootstrap, one stable lease, exact tombstones and cached cleanup prevent caller
        cancellation or repeated ensure/dispose from creating a second lifetime. A clean invisible
        rollback allows a new later attempt; cleanup failure retains quarantine.
    D3  Prepared snapshots replay immediately
        Direct prepared actor subscriptions replay the current prepared snapshot immediately and
        continue through activation. The user explicitly selected this timing. React render still
        creates no live subscription or Runtime resources; @plan.ReactAndHosts.O2 applies.
    D4  Terminal teardown can retire a causally blocked lane
        The user selected bounded terminal cleanup over waiting for upstream recovery. @O5 may
        retire the blocked refresh and queued work, settle private acknowledgments once, and finish
        disposal. Ordinary suspension still drains FIFO; ordinary events never overtake the head.
        @V5 and @plan.StoriesAndModels.V3 exercise this combined boundary.

constraints
    C1  No substitute authority or dynamic universe
        Exclude automatic/dynamic roots, runtime.actor, runtime machine/service/route registration,
        family lookup, child actor equivalents, generic registries, machine.provide, XState setup,
        public boot/decode/hydrate/dehydrate, public ManagedRuntime and acknowledged dispatch.
        Also exclude duplicate stores, actor registries, mailboxes, schedulers, evidence histories
        and hidden cancellation channels. Async module import before construction or a separately
        scoped lazy application island does not expand a live graph.
    C2  Private composition stays replaceable
        Exact shell phases, service/Layer topology, cell/queue/helper choices and unobservable
        reservation micro-order are not public compatibility. Inert construction, complete typed
        closure, singular lifetime ownership, causal order, atomic cuts and reverse cleanup bind
        every implementation. Effect execution edges belong to @plan.ReactAndHosts.O4.
verification
    V1  Closed graph, identity and inert compilation
        Establish @O1 with exact App.M inference and collisions across property names, IDs, values
        and owners. Check exact stable actor-ref bytes for ASCII and multibyte IDs; each prefix
        equals that segment's UTF-8 byte length. Construction creates no actor/work. Rename tooling
        ownership: artifact comparison fails closed while durable machine/actor identity stays
        unchanged.

        Reject live graph growth. Planned checks: rewrite app/module owner tests and packed type
        fixtures; not executed here.

        Candidate check: [app tests](../../../packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/app/test/app.test.ts).
        The complete cases above remain planned; this link is not a coverage or execution claim.
    V2  Requirements and provider type closure
        Establish @O2 with small/medium isolated consumers, raw descriptor R, Scope removal,
        cross-module union, complete/superset providers and fixture identity overrides. For inferred
        fixture, Story and model values, assert exact requirement unions, covariance and removal of
        only Flow-owned Scope. Complete constructor fixture closure must not erase those carriers.
        Reject missing/remaining requirements, duplicates and recursive expansion without excessive
        instantiation. Under strict/isolated declarations, provider construction runs no acquisition
        and preserves all exact channels without any/assertion erasure. Planned checks: rewrite
        check:types, Implementation tests and packed consumers; not executed for this proposal.

        Candidate check: [Implementation 
        tests](../../../packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/implementation/test/implementation.test.ts).
        The complete cases above remain planned; this link is not a coverage or execution claim.
    V3  Shared readiness and bootstrap cancellation
        Establish @O3 by controlling detached decode, acquisition, installation and activation.
        Assert the selected order and zero handle/evidence escape on invalid graphs, codecs, closure
        or headroom. Race two ready waiters, interrupt one, then complete acquisition: other
        succeeds from one bootstrap.

        Race disposal: late success cannot reopen admission. Typed failure, defect and interruption
        cache once; rollback finalizes acquired owners in reverse order. Planned checks: production
        readiness/persistence integration; no completed proof is claimed.

        Candidate check: [readiness 
        tests](../../../packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/readiness.test.ts).
        The complete cases above remain planned; this link is not a coverage or execution claim.
    V4  Shared ensure, clean retry and authority negatives
        Establish @O4 with concurrent differing inputs and compatible bindings: all join one lease,
        first accepted input initializes once. Conflicting fixed bindings reject unchanged. Inject
        an unpublished failure with clean rollback: concurrent callers share its failure; a later
        ensure with new input succeeds as a new attempt.

        Post-visibility failure uses disposal. get never creates/leases; create always yields
        distinct opaque refs. Missing/foreign/local/ tombstoned refs and partial admission fail
        before mutation/work. Planned production admission/lease tests plus type fixtures; unbuilt
        for this proposed full matrix.

        Candidate check: [admission 
        tests](../../../packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/actor-admission.test.ts).
        The complete cases above remain planned; this link is not a coverage or execution claim.
    V5  Lifecycle, dependency races and failed cleanup
        Establish @O5 by blocking a provider's disposal with active and suspended dependents and
        exact slot diagnostics. Race new consumer admission with provider close; neither a dangling
        edge nor post-close lease escapes. Suspend after queued commands: drain FIFO, settle queued
        finite work without adapter start, finalize once, preserve identity/deadlines.

        Resume after finalizers without replay. Cleanup defect blocks resume. Failed stable cleanup
        quarantines lookup/ensure/repeated disposal with the same failure and no rerun.

        Leave an upstream permanently suspended with a consumer's context wakeup blocked at the
        head and an accepted ordinary event behind it. Ordinary suspension stays pending without
        overtaking. Then request consumer terminal disposal, and separately Runtime/Story shutdown.
        The blocked refresh and queued event retire without executing the event or starting work.
        Each private acknowledgment settles once as terminal failure. Cleanup and terminal evidence
        drain complete without upstream recovery; prior domain truth remains intact. Include an
        actual non-abortable adapter separately; its required finalizer may still delay cleanup.

        Runtime teardown is reverse-dependency ordered and drains evidence. Planned production
        lifecycle tests; unbuilt.

        Candidate check: [lifecycle 
        tests](../../../packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/lifecycle.test.ts).
        The complete cases above remain planned; this link is not a coverage or execution claim.
    V6  Causal context, equal settlement and capture debt
        Establish @O6 with provider/consumer chains and a diamond. Delay one branch: the consumer
        cannot publish a mixed new/old causal cut; independently settled consumers may publish at
        different times. Settle one branch equal-valued: debt clears without a value-change event.

        Queue a context wakeup, publish newer providers, then execute: apply latest complete truth.
        A blocked head holds later ordinary events. Throw a consumer selector during provider
        publication. Keep provider/sibling settlement, retained context, issue/event behavior, and
        retry trigger pending under @Q1; the current candidate is not an approved pass condition.

        Baseline stays silent. Suspended debt rejects capture until explicit recovery. Planned
        controlled production context/capture tests and exact selector/binding type fixtures;
        unbuilt.

        Candidate check: 
        [tests](../../../packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/context.test.ts).
        The complete cases above remain planned; this link is not a coverage or execution claim.
    V7  Atomic publication, current snapshots and safe termination
        Establish @O7 by observing actor/store together through live, Story and passive readers. No
        store-before-actor cut, user Effect-before-publication, inline sink, torn read or mutable
        past reader is visible. Ack follows evidence acceptance without waiting for effects/sinks.

        During turn processing, issue two accepted public sends reentrantly. Pass when they execute
        FIFO after the current decision and each private acknowledgment settles exactly once after
        its decision; publishing acknowledgments follow evidence acceptance.

        Slow readers may skip but always receive a complete current cut. Subscribe while prepared:
        replay prepared immediately and continue activation; suspended/disposed replay and complete.
        Saturate queues: AdmissionOverloaded changes no occurrence/state/work.

        Inject near-limit counters at admission/restore: reserve terminal capacity or reject before
        mutation; terminal cleanup remains publishable. Fatal closure settles every private
        acknowledgment once. Planned publication, snapshot, queue and evidence tests; no executable
        receipt claimed.


        Candidate check: [publication evidence 
        tests](../../../packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/actor-evidence.test.ts).
        The complete cases above remain planned; this link is not a coverage or execution claim.

questions
    Q1  What isolation and retry policy applies to consumer selector defects?
        @plan.Runtime.O4 places pre-commit selector defects under prior-truth and issue-only
        handling, without retrying issue-only work. @plan.Runtime.V5 expects reruns on matching
        publications, but neither defines provider/sibling commits or retained consumer context
        when a provider-wave selector throws.

        Does one consumer defect leave provider and sibling updates committed, retain its
        last valid context, publish issue-only truth without a context event, and retry only after
        a newer provider revision? O6 currently presents this behavior as a candidate.
